    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Projection inferences are inferences about speaker commitment to content embedded that project through an entailment-canceling operator. In this thesis, we investigate projection inferences in polar interrogatives with clause-embedding predicates (e.g., Does John know that Julian dances salsa?). Inferred speaker belief regarding whether Julian dances salsa is modulated by multiple factors, including the predicate used, interlocutors' prior beliefs about the content, and the content's at-issueness. In a behavioral study, we investigate the beliefs about contents embedded under the predicates “think” and “know” that listeners ascribe to the speaker and a potential attitude holder (John). We propose three different probabilistic models couched in the Rational Speech Act (RSA) framework, in an attempt to provide a unified computational account of projection inferences about content embedded under clause-embedding predicates. The empirical data are used to infer the parameters and evaluate the model predictions. The predictions of each model mirror some, but not all, of the qualitative empirical patterns. This is a step towards a systematic analysis of projection inferences using probabilistic pragmatic models.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Pan, D. (2023). Projection inferences with clause-embedding predicates in RSA models. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/sx158jh3545. https://doi.org/10.25740/sx158jh3545.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
